## 2. Walkthrough & Exam Drills

### Drill 1: Imperative vs. Declarative Pod Creation (`01-imperative-declarative/`)

1. **Fast Imperative Creation**:

   During the exam, time is precious. Use `kubectl run` to generate running Pods without writing YAML by hand:

   ```bash
   # Imperative creation with port, env vars, and labels
   kubectl run cka-lab-web-service \
     --image=nginx:alpine \
     --port=80 \
     --env="APP_ENV=production" \
     --env="LOG_LEVEL=info" \
     --labels="tier=frontend,app=cka-lab-web-service"
   ```

1. **Generating Declarative Boilerplate**:

   Use `--dry-run=client -o yaml` to generate clean starter YAML:

   ```bash
   kubectl run cka-lab-quick-task \
     --image=busybox:1.36.1 \
     --restart=Never \
     --dry-run=client -o yaml \
     -- /bin/sh -c 'echo "Task completed"' > task.yaml
   ```

1. **Declarative Customization**:

   Apply declarative manifests specifying command and argument overrides:

   ```bash
   kubectl apply -f {{ cka_lab_pods_dir }}/01-imperative-declarative/cka-lab-batch-task-pod.yaml
   kubectl get pod cka-lab-batch-task
   ```

### Drill 2: Lifecycle Phases & Restart Policies (`02-lifecycle-and-restarts/`)

1. **Understand Pod Phases vs Container States**:
   - **Pod Phase**: High-level cluster status (`Pending`, `Running`, `Succeeded`, `Failed`, `Unknown`).
   - **Container State**: Individual container status (`Waiting`, `Running`, `Terminated`).

1. **Finite Workloads (`restartPolicy: Never`)**:

   Deploy a Pod meant to run once and terminate:

   ```bash
   kubectl apply -f {{ cka_lab_pods_dir }}/02-lifecycle-and-restarts/cka-lab-finite-worker-pod.yaml
   # Observe transition from Running -> Succeeded (Completed)
   kubectl get pod cka-lab-finite-worker -w
   ```

1. **Failing Workloads (`CrashLoopBackOff`)**:

   Deploy a Pod with a failing command under `restartPolicy: OnFailure`:

   ```bash
   kubectl apply -f {{ cka_lab_pods_dir }}/02-lifecycle-and-restarts/cka-lab-failing-worker-pod.yaml
   # Observe restart count increment and CrashLoopBackOff state
   kubectl get pod cka-lab-failing-worker
   kubectl describe pod cka-lab-failing-worker | grep -A 5 "Last State:"
   ```

### Drill 3: Observability & Diagnostics (`03-observability-exec/`)

1. Deploy the telemetry emitter Pod:

   ```bash
   kubectl apply -f {{ cka_lab_pods_dir }}/03-observability-exec/cka-lab-telemetry-pod.yaml
   ```

1. **Reading Logs**:

   ```bash
   # Retrieve recent container logs
   kubectl logs cka-lab-telemetry-app --tail=10

   # Stream logs in real time
   kubectl logs -f cka-lab-telemetry-app

   # Inspect previous container instance after a crash
   kubectl logs cka-lab-failing-worker -p
   ```

1. **Executing Commands Inside Containers**:

   ```bash
   # Non-interactive command execution
   kubectl exec cka-lab-telemetry-app -- env
   kubectl exec cka-lab-telemetry-app -- ps aux

   # Interactive shell
   kubectl exec -it cka-lab-telemetry-app -- /bin/sh
   ```

### Drill 4: Ephemeral Troubleshooting Pods & Networking (`04-ephemeral-networking/`)

1. Deploy the HTTP echo server:

   ```bash
   kubectl apply -f {{ cka_lab_pods_dir }}/04-ephemeral-networking/cka-lab-echo-server-pod.yaml
   ```

1. Retrieve the Pod's cluster IP:

   ```bash
   POD_IP=$(kubectl get pod cka-lab-echo-server -o jsonpath='{.status.podIP}')
   echo "Echo server IP: ${POD_IP}"
   ```

1. Launch a temporary ephemeral Pod to probe the HTTP endpoint:

   ```bash
   # --rm automatically cleans up the Pod upon completion
   kubectl run cka-lab-ephemeral-probe \
     --image=busybox:1.36.1 \
     --rm -it --restart=Never \
     -- wget -qO- "http://${POD_IP}:80"
   ```

### Drill 5: Handling Pod Spec Immutability (`05-spec-immutability/`)

1. Deploy version 1 of the application:

   ```bash
   kubectl apply -f {{ cka_lab_pods_dir }}/05-spec-immutability/cka-lab-immutable-app-v1.yaml
   ```

1. Attempting to change container ports or commands on a live Pod will fail:

   ```bash
   # Kubernetes returns: Pod updates may not change fields other than ...
   kubectl apply -f {{ cka_lab_pods_dir }}/05-spec-immutability/cka-lab-immutable-app-v2.yaml
   ```

1. **The CKA Solution: Force Replacement**:

   ```bash
   # Atomically deletes the old Pod and instantiates the new spec
   kubectl replace --force -f {{ cka_lab_pods_dir }}/05-spec-immutability/cka-lab-immutable-app-v2.yaml
   kubectl get pod cka-lab-immutable-app
   ```

### Drill 6: Namespaces & Context Preferences (`06-namespaces-context/`)

1. Create a dedicated practice namespace:

   ```bash
   kubectl apply -f {{ cka_lab_pods_dir }}/06-namespaces-context/cka-lab-namespace.yaml
   kubectl get namespaces
   ```

1. Run a workload directly within that namespace:

   ```bash
   kubectl run cka-lab-staging-worker \
     --image=busybox:1.36.1 \
     --namespace={{ cka_lab_pods_staging_namespace }} \
     -- /bin/sh -c 'while true; do sleep 5; done'
   ```

1. **Permanently Set Default Context Namespace Preference**:

   Avoid typing `-n <namespace>` on every command by binding your current context:

   ```bash
   # Set active namespace preference
   kubectl config set-context --current --namespace={{ cka_lab_pods_staging_namespace }}

   # Verify active preference
   kubectl config view --minify | grep namespace:

   # Now queries automatically target cka-lab-staging
   kubectl get pods

   # Reset preference back to default namespace
   kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=default
   ```

1. **Cascading Deletion**:

   Deleting the namespace automatically deletes all resources hosted inside it:

   ```bash
   kubectl delete namespace {{ cka_lab_pods_staging_namespace }}
   ```

